# ComposeUX Widget Primitives [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

> Six primitives, built as **components you fork**, not code widgets that lock the look. Each is a pixel-stack of existing primitives wired with bindings + the expression DSL, so it renders on every backend for free and its default `.compose.yaml` is the thing you clone and restyle. Pre-vis (ratified aesthetic): `ux/widget-primitives-previs.v1.html`.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-PRIM-WHY] Composites, Not Code Widgets

- decision (David 2026-07-09): these six ship as reusable **components** (`components:` map + `{use: …}`, [14-composer-components]), NOT new engine `Widget` subclasses.
- reasoning: David will *rarely* use the default look (e.g. BSG progress bars need a bespoke, consistent feel) — so the default's value is being **inspectable and forkable**. A component is an author-readable `.compose.yaml` composition you clone and restyle; a code widget hides its rendering in Python/C++ and locks the look. Composites also render on every backend for free (they compose primitives that already implement the Canvas contract) and reuse the binding/DSL/component systems already built.
- consequence: the "default implementation" IS the deliverable artifact — seeing it is the point. Custom variants are forks that keep the shared tokens ([CXC-PRIM-TOKENS]) for family consistency and change the rest.
- note: existing code widgets that overlap (`power_toggle` ≈ switch, `checkbox`, `tab`) are NOT retired; these composites are the forkable family beside them.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

---

## 2. Ratified Default Aesthetic

### [CXC-PRIM-TOKENS] The Shared Token Set (ratified from the pre-vis, David 2026-07-09)

- surfaces: card/panel over an inset track; cool-biased neutrals.
- accent = **the interactive/active state only** (fill, thumb, on-state, focus) — a single measured teal, deliberately not a neon pop. Dark `#34c3d0`; light `#159aa8`.
- semantic (good/warn/crit) is a SEPARATE axis from the accent — a progress bar's `warn` color is a threshold binding, never the accent.
- conventions: corner radius **4px**; thumbs are pill/circular; tracks **6–10px**; value labels in **mono**; both light+dark themed.
- rule: a fork keeps these tokens for a consistent family and restyles the rest. The tokens are the contract that makes bespoke variants still look related.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

---

## 3. The Catalog

### [CXC-PRIM-CATALOG] Six Primitives

Each is a `components:` entry (`params` + `defaults` + `widget` template); `{{param}}` resolves at load, `ns('…')`/`${ns}` at runtime.

- **progress_bar** (pure composite): track rect + fill rect. `binds`: value → `fill.width = expr(clamp(ns('{{value_ns}}'),0,1)*{{W}})`. `params`: value_ns, height, radius, color, warn_at, show_label. Variants: determinate + indeterminate (a looping behavior).
- **switch** (pure composite): bg pill + knob. `binds`: state (binary) ⇄ tap→toggle; `knob.x = expr(state ? on : off)`. `params`: state_ns, on_color, labels, momentary.
- **numpad** (pure composite): grid of `button` components (0–9, CLR, ↵). `binds`: each key → publish char to a target text namespace. `params`: target_ns, allow_decimal, on_enter.
- **keyboard** (pure composite): grid of `button` components (QWERTY + shift/backspace/space as param keys) editing a text namespace a `text_input` consumes. `params`: layout, target_ns, shift_mode, keycaps.
- **slider** (composite + [CXC-PRIM-DRAGVAL]): track + fill + thumb. `binds`: value ⇄ drag; `thumb.x = expr(ns('{{value_ns}}')*{{W}})`. `params`: value_ns, min, max, step, orient, live_or_on_release.
- **dropdown** (composite + [CXC-PRIM-POPUP]): closed field (label + chevron) + popup item list. `binds`: selected ⇄ item tap → publish + dismiss. `params`: selected_ns, options[], placeholder, max_visible.
- **segment_bar** (composite + [CXC-PRIM-REPEAT], David 2026-07-09): an N-segment LED bargraph (the classic 10-segment look — discrete lit segments, zoned green/yellow/red). `binds`: value → each segment lit/dim + zoned color via one per-segment expression. `params`: value_ns, segments (N), green (count), yellow (count) [red = N−green−yellow], orient (horizontal/vertical), colors (green/yellow/red hex, defaulting to the semantic tokens), gap. Reference image: a 10-seg bar, 6 green / 2 yellow / 2 red, horizontal, on a dark bezel.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-FADER] The Linear Fill / Fader — a READOUT, so a Code Style Mode

- what: a linear FILL / FADER gauge — an inset track the bound value fills as a fraction of `[min_val, max_val]`. `orientation: vertical` (default) fills BOTTOM->TOP (a throttle lever / VU meter); `orientation: horizontal` fills LEFT->RIGHT (a level meter). It draws a bright level-cap line at the fill edge, a min/max scale, and a big raw-value readout; the fill warms teal->amber->red on `warning_threshold`/`danger_threshold` ([CXC-PRIM-TOKENS]: accent is the active fill, semantic is the separate threshold axis).
- delivered AS: `style: "bar"` on the EXISTING `GaugeWidget` (`python/composeio/render/widgets/gauge.py`) — one more style MODE beside `dial` and `semicircle`, per [CXC-ICV-GAUGE]'s ratified "generalize, don't fork". NOT a new engine class; NOT a `components:` composite. API mirrors the dial exactly: `namespace`, `min_val`, `max_val`, `label`, `warning_threshold`/`danger_threshold`, plus `orientation`. Namespace binding, the `on_bus_state` float/dict hook, the registry entry, and the parser branch are all reused unchanged.
- why a code widget here (and NOT one of the six [CXC-PRIM-WHY] composites): the six are INPUT primitives (slider/switch/dropdown/…) whose value IS being inspectable-and-forkable, because David will rarely use their default look. A fader is a READOUT in the gauge family (dial/semicircle/bar) — its value is a stable, `namespace:`-bound API, not a look to fork. Forking it as a rect-and-DSL composite is exactly the brittleness this fact retires (see the change record). So it earns the gauge's code style-mode, while the input primitives stay composites — the discipline splits on input-to-fork vs. readout-with-a-stable-API, not on "code vs. composite" as a blanket rule.
- change: "The dual-throttle pot-calibration bar is a first-class namespace-bound widget (`gauge` `style: bar`), not a hand-composed scene fragment."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "The throttle calibration view was a hand-authored `pixel_stack` of `rect` children whose fill HEIGHT was driven by inline `ns('throttle/left')`-style geometry expressions — a one-off per scene, brittle to DSL string-literal quoting, and not reusable."
  - reasoning: "David asked whether the calibration bar 'should have its own namespace and be a real ComposeUX widget'. It should: a fill/fader is a readout gauge, and the gauge already carries the exact clean API (`namespace`/`min_val`/`max_val`/`label`/thresholds) plus its bus hook and cross-backend Canvas-contract draw. Adding `style: bar` reuses all of it and follows [CXC-ICV-GAUGE] ('generalize, don't fork'); the scene becomes two `namespace:`-bound widgets + a title with zero hand-wired `ns()` geometry."
  - rejected_alternative: "A new `fader`/`bar` engine Widget subclass — rejected: it would duplicate the gauge's value/threshold/bus API for no gain and split a readout family across two classes. Also rejected: a `components:` composite in the [CXC-PRIM-CATALOG] family — that is the correct home for a FORKABLE input, but re-introduces the exact rect+DSL-geometry brittleness this change retires, for a widget whose value is its bound API, not its look."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That a fader's default look will be forked as often as a slider's. If a scene needs a bespoke fader look, it forks the drawing (a gauge style variant) — the tokens ([CXC-PRIM-TOKENS]) still bind the family; the namespace API is unchanged."
  - implication: "The gauge is now the single readout home for dial + semicircle + linear-fill; `orientation` is the fader's axis/origin selector. The demo scene is `render/examples/calib_throttle.compose.yaml`."
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-LEDBAR] The Segmented LED Level-Meter — a READOUT, so a Code Style Mode

- what: an LM3914-style segmented LED bar-graph readout — a discrete row/column of `segments` LED elements on a near-black bezel, driven by a `namespace:`-bound value over `[min_val, max_val]`. Segment i (1..N) LIGHTS when `value >= min_val + (i/N)*(max_val - min_val)` (deciles for N=10); a lit segment glows its palette color, an unlit one is a dim (~16% luminance) HUE-TRUE version of the SAME color on the bezel, so an off-LED reads colored-but-dark, never black. The palette IS the zone coloring (default blue idle -> green cruise -> yellow caution -> red max, sampled from a real module), so `warning_threshold`/`danger_threshold` are IRRELEVANT here and untouched — contrast the fader ([CXC-PRIM-FADER]), whose fill warms on them.
- delivered AS: `style: "led_bar"` on the EXISTING `GaugeWidget` (`python/composeio/render/widgets/gauge.py`, `_draw_led_bar`) — one more style MODE beside `dial`, `semicircle`, and `bar` ([CXC-PRIM-FADER]), per [CXC-ICV-GAUGE]'s "generalize, don't fork". NOT a new class; NOT the forkable `segment_bar` composite ([CXC-PRIM-CATALOG]). Same readout-with-a-stable-API discipline as the fader ([CXC-PRIM-FADER] change record): a segmented meter's value is a `namespace:`-bound API, not a look to fork — so it earns the gauge's code style-mode, while the INPUT primitives ([CXC-PRIM-WHY]) stay forkable composites.
- reuse-by-reference (extends, never forks): the `namespace`/`min_val`/`max_val`/`label` API, the `on_bus_state` float/dict hook, the registry entry, the `measure`/`draw` style routing, and the `_frac`/`_hex_to_rgb` helpers are reused UNCHANGED. Built from `fill_round_rect`/`fill_circle` ONLY (R1: decompose to the Canvas contract, [CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT]) so it renders identically on Skia, the browser, and the CYD band renderer — the prerequisite that lets [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY] be satisfiable by a mechanical back-end port. This node CONFIRMS the shipped baseline and ADDS five configuration params; it forks nothing and keeps the current render byte-identical at the defaults.

**EXISTING params (already implemented — confirm/formalize, do NOT re-build):**

| Param | Type | Default | Semantics |
|-------|------|---------|-----------|
| `segments` | int | `10` | LED count N; clamped `>= 1` (bad input -> 10). |
| `segment_colors` | list[hex] | engine palette (`#0236FE` / `#01FF03`x4 / `#FFEB01`x3 / `#FE0002`x2) | per-LED color; a SHORT list REPEATS its last entry to length N; bad hex -> mid-grey (never breaks the screen). |
| `orientation` | `"vertical"`\|`"horizontal"` | `"vertical"` | the AXIS the bar runs along: vertical = stacked column; horizontal = row. |
| `reverse` | bool | `false` | flips which END is segment-1 / the fill origin ALONG the orientation axis. |
| lit/unlit model | — | — | seg i lit iff `value >= min_val+(i/N)*range`; unlit = `color x 0.16` (`_LED_DIM_FACTOR`); lit gains a brighter inner lens core (`_LED_CORE_LIFT` 0.30). |

**NEW params (the enhancement — TO ADD, David 2026-07-29). Defaults chosen backward-compatible:**

| Param | Type | Default | Semantics | Compliance check |
|-------|------|---------|-----------|------------------|
| `segment_shape` | `"rectangular"`\|`"square"`\|`"round"` | `"rectangular"` | LED element geometry. `rectangular` = rounded rect (current impl); `square` = a square, side = `min(short,long)` slot extent, centered in its slot; `round` = a circle, diameter = `min` slot extent, centered. | each shape renders; `rectangular` is byte-identical to the pre-enhancement output. |
| `segment_long_axis` | `"across"`\|`"along"` | `"across"` | RECTANGULAR ONLY. `across` (default) = the rect's LONG axis lies ACROSS the fill axis (wide LEDs stacked / tall LEDs in a row — current geometry). `along` = the rects' SMALL ends are adjacent, long axis ALONG the fill direction. Ignored for `square`/`round`. | with `along`, adjacent rects meet on their short edges; `across` reproduces current geometry. |
| `segment_gap` | float px | `4.0` | space between adjacent LEDs along the fill axis (was the hardcoded `gap = 4.0`). `>= 0`. | `4` reproduces current spacing; `0` abuts segments; other values change the gap measurably. |
| `padding` | float px | `8.0` | inset from the widget rect to the module bezel on ALL sides (was the hardcoded `pad = 8.0`). `>= 0`. | `8` reproduces current inset; larger shrinks the bezel symmetrically. |
| `min_segment_size` | float px | `0.0` (disabled) | floor on the LED's SHORT-axis extent (rect short side / square side / circle diameter). `0` = no floor (backward-compatible). Overflow recovery when `>0` and the layout cannot give every segment `>=` this: (1) shrink `segment_gap` toward a 1px floor; (2) if still infeasible, reduce the RENDERED count to the largest `n'` with `(strip_extent - (n'+1)*gap)/n' >= min_segment_size`, rescaling the lit deciles + palette sampling to `n'`. NEVER render below the floor; NEVER overflow the rect. | at `0` output is unchanged; with a floor on an undersized rect, every rendered LED `>= floor` and the module stays within its rect. |

- module flip (David's item d) — RECONCILED onto the existing two knobs; NO third module-mirror param exists or is needed:
  - VERTICAL flip (mirror top<->bottom) of a column = `orientation: vertical` + toggle `reverse`.
  - HORIZONTAL flip (mirror left<->right) of a row = `orientation: horizontal` + toggle `reverse`.
  - Rotate the module 90 deg (change which axis it runs along) = change `orientation`.
  - The PERPENDICULAR mirror (a column mirrored left<->right, or a row mirrored top<->bottom) is a visual IDENTITY (the strip is symmetric across that axis), so it needs no knob.
  - Net: `orientation` picks the long axis, `reverse` picks the origin end; together they enumerate every distinct module presentation. Item (d) is satisfied by the existing knobs — the spec adds NO flip param. (Flagged for architect ratify.)
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

---

## 4. The Two New Interaction Seams

### [CXC-PRIM-DRAGVAL] Drag-to-Value (the slider needs this)

- gap: the engine already delivers `on_pan_start/on_pan/on_pan_end` to widgets ([CXC-RM-M1]) and publishes tap/long_press interactions, but there is no primitive that converts a drag into a **normalized value published to a namespace**.
- contract: a small, reusable interaction behavior — a draggable region maps pointer position along its axis to a `[min,max]` value (respecting `step`) and publishes it to a namespace. Modes: **live** (publish on every `on_pan`) or **on_release** (publish once on `on_pan_end`). Deterministic + bus-native: an Intelligence can "drag" by publishing the value directly, identical to a finger.
- reuse: this is the same "state on the bus, behavior in the widget" model; the slider's thumb position is a normal bound expression on the published value, so the visual is pure composite — only the *input* (pan→publish) is the new seam.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-REPEAT] The `repeat` Generator (the segment_bar needs this; keyboard/numpad want it)

- gap: the component expander ([14-composer-components]) substitutes `{{param}}` into a FIXED template — it cannot emit a *variable* number of children from a count param. An N-segment bargraph, and cleanly-generated key grids, need "clone this child N times, indexed."
- decision: build a **code widget for this? NO** — that would be the first primitive to break define-once (needing a separate C++/CYD implementation) and would lock the look. Instead add a minimal **`repeat` construct** to the expander: a child carrying `repeat: <count> as: <name>` is unrolled at load into `count` concrete children, substituting the integer index token (`{{i}}`) 0..count-1 (and `{{count}}`). Nothing more — no load-time arithmetic engine.
- key-insight (why it stays tiny): the **runtime DSL already exists** ([SPEC:CXC-DSL]), so ALL per-index logic lives in a normal bound expression, not the generator. The generator only substitutes the integer index as a literal; the expression does the math. A segment's color is one binding: `fill_color: { expr: "ns('{{value_ns}}') * {{count}} >= {{i}} + 1 ? ({{i}} < {{green}} ? '<greenhex>' : {{i}} < {{green}}+{{yellow}} ? '<yellowhex>' : '<redhex>') : '<dimhex>'" }` — lit/dim AND green/yellow/red zone in one expression, index/counts substituted as literals at load. (A `rect` binds `fill_color`, not `color` — the DSL result `'#RRGGBB'` is coerced to RGBA by the site since `fill_color ∈ COLOR_ATTRS`.) This is a direct dividend of building the DSL before the widgets.
- reuse: `repeat` generalizes — keyboard/numpad key grids become generated rows rather than hand-listed (a follow-on refactor, not required now); tick marks, gauge ticks, any repeating structure.
- composition: `repeat` runs in the same load-time expansion pass as `{{param}}`/`use:`; nested `repeat` and `repeat` inside a `use:` component must compose. Layout keys ([14] `_LAYOUT_KEYS`) on the repeated child apply per-copy so the container lays them out (a horizontal/vertical row of segments).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-POPUP] Popup-List Overlay (the dropdown needs this)

- gap: dropdown needs a transient overlay list with select + outside-dismiss. Overlay mechanics already exist (the Command arm-confirm + BusMonitor pop-out use `DialogWidget`); what's missing is a **list-select popup** as a reusable interaction.
- contract: a popup surface anchored to the field, rendering an item list; an item tap publishes the selected value to a namespace and dismisses; an outside tap / escape dismisses without change. Reuse the existing dialog/overlay layer rather than inventing a second overlay system.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

---

## 5. Tier Parity (David's durable ruling, 2026-07-29)

### [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY] A Capability Is Not Done Until It Renders on the Embedded Tier

- principle: a UX capability built on the Python/Skia tier MUST ALSO be renderable on ESP32-class embedded devices, across the SAME narrow-waist Canvas contract ([CXC-R-DP-2]: rendering target is a transport-level detail; [CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT]), both back-ends. A widget or gauge style is NOT "done" as a platform capability until it renders on the embedded tier ([SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]) OR is EXPLICITLY scoped console-only with a recorded reason.
- why: this is the render-engineer's "decompose to the contract, every backend gets it" (R1/R3) turned into a COMPLETION BAR. A capability drawn with ONLY Canvas-contract primitives is portable by construction; one that reaches past the contract (Skia-only calls, platform widgets) silently strands the embedded tier. Define-once ([CXC-PRIM-WHY]: composites render on every backend for free; the "define once, actuate many" discipline of [CXC-ER-HAL]) is the same idea; tier-parity makes it a GATE, not an aspiration.
- motivating instance: `led_bar` ([CXC-PRIM-LEDBAR]) and the `bar`/fader gauge style ([CXC-PRIM-FADER]) shipped Python-only; the round Qualia dradis cannot use them until the C++ embedded-tier port lands ([SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE] R-slices). Both are ALREADY contract-clean (built from `fill_round_rect`/`fill_circle`/`draw_line` only), so the port is a mechanical back-end pass, not a redesign — exactly the property tier-parity requires up front.
- the bar (definition-of-done): each new UX capability carries a tier line — (a) renders on Python/Skia AND the C++ embedded back-end over the one Canvas contract, OR (b) an explicit `console-only:` scope with a reason. "Renders on Skia" alone is a DRAFT capability, not a landed platform one.
- escape hatch: console-only is legitimate (some surfaces are desktop-console-specific), but it is DECLARED and REASONED, never a silent omission — the same preserve-and-flag discipline the format uses for a dangling edge.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

---

## 6. The Carousel — a Touch Scale-Carousel (cover-flow)

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL] The carousel widget — a continuous-position, touch-driven cover-flow

- what: a horizontal SCALE-CAROUSEL (cover-flow) — a strip of tiles where a CENTER tile renders full-size and lifted above its neighbors, tiles to either side shrink toward `side_scale` with distance, and a horizontal touch-swipe scrolls the strip with snap-to-tile + edge rubber-band. It is the ComposePlatform equivalent of the CTk launcher's `CarouselView` (`/Maker/launcher/widgets/carousel.py`), re-expressed in the "state on the bus, behavior in the widget" model. A dot-indicator row below shows one dot per tile with the centered one highlighted.
- name (generic, per generalize-names-back): the widget is `carousel` — NOT `launcher_carousel`. It is a general navigation/browse widget; a launcher is one caller. Its param/binding surface names positions and tiles, never apps.
- the single source of truth is a continuous float `position` (`0.0` = the first tile centered; `1.0` = the second centered; fractional = mid-scroll). EVERYTHING renders from `position`: a tile's x, a tile's scale, the active dot, and `current_index = round(position)`. `position` lives on the bus (a widget-scoped namespace) so the render is a pure function of a bus value and the widget is drivable/observable headlessly — [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND].
- delivered AS: a composite VISUAL (a `pixel_stack` strip of per-tile children whose `x`/scale are bound expressions on `position`, using the bindable pixel-stack geometry landed in [SPEC:CXC-BIND]) + ONE new interaction-controller seam — the **swipe-carousel controller** ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH]) — that maps a horizontal pan to `position` with tap/drag discrimination, snap, and edge-elastic. This mirrors the slider's split ([CXC-PRIM-DRAGVAL]: the visual is a pure bound composite; only the input→publish is the new seam), extended with a snap ANIMATION (the drag-value seam publishes a value; the carousel controller additionally animates `position` toward a settled target).
- why a seam (and not one of the pure composites [CXC-PRIM-WHY], nor a code Widget that locks a look): the carousel is an INPUT primitive whose value (`position`/`current_index`) IS a bus-observable API, and whose LOOK (tile size, scale falloff, dots, tokens) is meant to be forked — so it stays in the composite family. But its behavior (a drag/snap state machine + an ease-out animation loop) cannot live in a per-frame DSL expression, so it earns a small interaction-controller seam exactly as the slider earned [CXC-PRIM-DRAGVAL]. The seam is the ONLY new engine code; the drawing is contract-clean composite.
- shared tokens: the dot indicator and any chrome keep [CXC-PRIM-TOKENS] — the active dot defaults to the accent, inactive to a dim neutral; a fork restyles the rest.
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-MODEL], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TILES], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-PARAMS], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TIER], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-VERIFY]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-MODEL] The position model + cover-flow geometry (the render is a pure function of `position`)

- definition: given a continuous `position`, a spacing `S` (`spacing`, center-to-center px), a widget width `W`, and per-tile index `i` (0-based, list order), the widget MUST render tile `i` at:
  - x (center of the tile): `x_i = W/2 + (i − position) * S`. The tile is drawn centered on `x_i`.
  - scale (linear falloff with distance, CLAMPED): `scale_i = max(side_scale, center_scale − (center_scale − side_scale) * min(1, |i − position|))`. So the centered tile is `center_scale`; a tile a full pane or more away is `side_scale`; between, it interpolates linearly. Defaults `center_scale = 1.0`, `side_scale = 0.5` ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-PARAMS]).
  - z-order: the tile nearest center (`round(position)`) MUST be lifted ABOVE its neighbors (it overlaps them at `center_scale`).
  - culling: a tile whose scaled box lies entirely outside `[0, W]` MUST NOT be drawn (off-screen cull), an optimization that MUST NOT change any on-screen tile's x/scale.
- `current_index`: `round(position)`. The dot row highlights `round(position)`; exactly one dot is active.
- requirement (testable): a headless render at `position ∈ {0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0}` MUST produce each on-screen tile's `x_i` and `scale_i` matching the two formulas above to within 0.5 px / 0.001 scale — this is the machine gate in [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-VERIFY].
- default clamp (no wrap): `position` and every snap target are clamped to `[0, n−1]` for `n` tiles; the carousel does NOT wrap. `wrap` is a param defaulting `false` ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-PARAMS]); the CTk source clamps, so `false` is the faithful default.
- scenario:
  - when: `n = 3`, `S = 425`, `W = 1080`, `position = 0.5`
  - then: tile 0 sits at `x = 540 + (0−0.5)*425 = 327.5`, tile 1 at `540 + 212.5 = 752.5`, and both render at `scale = max(0.5, 1.0 − 0.5*min(1,0.5)) = 0.75`
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH] The swipe-carousel controller — drag, tap-vs-drag, snap, elastic, state machine

- definition: a reusable interaction controller (the new seam; the carousel analogue of `DragValueController`, [CXC-PRIM-DRAGVAL]) that consumes the engine's `on_pan_start`/`on_pan`/`on_pan_end` + tap ([CXC-RM-M1]) and drives the single `position` value. It reuses the "behavior in the widget, state on the bus" model: it only ever WRITES `position` (and publishes the settled index), so an Intelligence publishing a target index moves the carousel identically to a finger ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]).
- drag tracks the finger 1:1: during a pan, `position = anchor_position + (−dx / S)` — dragging one full `S` of travel moves exactly one pane. The widget re-renders every pan event (the geometry of [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-MODEL] recomputed at the live `position`).
- tap vs drag (on release): a release is a TAP iff total drift `< tap_max_drift_px` (default 15 px) AND duration `< tap_max_duration_ms` (default 300 ms); a tap ACTIVATES the centered tile ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]). Otherwise the release is a SWIPE and runs the snap decision.
- snap decision (on a swipe release): move to the ADJACENT tile in the swipe direction iff `|dx| > snap_threshold * S` (default `0.15`) OR `|velocity| > flick_velocity` (default 400 px/s); else snap BACK to the origin index. The new target is `round(base) ± 1` clamped to `[0, n−1]` (`base` = the committed pre-drag target).
- NO momentum / inertia (load-bearing): a flick advances AT MOST ONE tile — velocity picks the DIRECTION, never the DISTANCE. There is no multi-tile fling. This is a deliberate divergence from OS carousels and MUST hold.
- snap animation: `position` eases from its release value to the integer target over `snap_duration_ms` (default 300 ms) using `snap_easing` (default ease-out-cubic, `1 − (1−t)³`). On completion `position` is set EXACTLY to the integer target and the settled index is published.
- edge elastic (rubber-band): while `position < 0` or `position > n−1` during a drag, the overshoot past the edge is multiplied by `elastic_factor` (default 0.30) so the strip resists past the first/last tile; on release it snaps back inside `[0, n−1]`.
- state machine (mutually exclusive): the controller is in exactly one of `idle` / `dragging` / `snapping`. Invariants that MUST hold: a new `on_pan_start` CANCELS an in-flight snap (`snapping → dragging`); a snap runs only from `idle`/post-release; both a COMMITTED target-position (the last settled/target integer) and the LIVE continuous `position` are tracked, and the drag offset is always applied relative to the committed target, never the live mid-animation value.
- OUT OF SCOPE (dropped secondary inputs, recorded so the omission is not silent): the CTk source also drives the carousel from a ROTARY ENCODER (`encoder_pulse`, pulses-per-pane tracking) and exposes `go_to`; the ComposePlatform widget specs TOUCH + the `position`/`goto` model ONLY. Keyboard arrow-nav is an OPTIONAL nicety a caller MAY wire to `goto_ns` (left/right = ∓1); the rotary encoder and any HA/hardware nav are DROPPED (not ported) — an encoder-driven variant, if ever wanted, publishes to `goto_ns` like any other peer and needs no widget change.
- scenario:
  - when: at rest on index 1, a finger drags 40 px left over 120 ms and releases (`S = 425`, so `|dx|=40 < 0.15*425 = 63.75`, low velocity)
  - then: below threshold → snap BACK to index 1; `position` eases to `1.0`; `current_index` stays 1
  - when: instead the finger flicks left 30 px in 40 ms at 900 px/s
  - then: `|velocity| 900 > 400` → advance ONE to index 2 (never two, no momentum); `position` eases to `2.0`
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-MODEL], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TILES] Two tile kinds — a static image, or a LIVE child widget

- definition: `tiles` is an ordered list; each entry is ONE of:
  - an IMAGE tile — `{ image: <path/asset> }` — a static icon/picture, drawn scaled per the carousel.
  - a LIVE-WIDGET tile — `{ widget: <use-ref or inline component> }` — a hosted, live child widget (the CTk `widget_factory` seam re-expressed). This is load-bearing: the ComposePlatform conversion renders a live `fiat_widget` and a `bsg_app` AS tiles, not stills — the tile's child keeps updating (bus-bound) while it sits in the carousel.
  - either entry MAY also carry `target:` (the activate-intent target for a center-tap, [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]) and `label:` (shown under the tile / used for the missing-image fallback).
- requirement: a missing/unresolved image tile MUST fall back to its `label` on a neutral swatch and MUST be logged — never a blank tile (preserve-and-flag, mirroring [CXC-LB-TILE]). A live-widget tile that fails to construct renders the same labeled-swatch fallback.
- requirement: image and live-widget tiles MUST occupy the SAME tile box (`tile_width` × `tile_height` at `center_scale`) and MUST scale by the SAME mechanism ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE]) — a caller MUST be able to mix image and live tiles in one carousel with no visual seam between the kinds.
- default tile box: `tile_width = 500`, `tile_height = 500` px (the unscaled center extent; CTk's `ELEMENT_BASE_W/H`), overridable ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-PARAMS]).
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE] DESIGN DECISION — scale ALL tiles uniformly via the render engine's canvas scale transform

- decision (architect, flagged for ratify): the ComposePlatform carousel scales EVERY tile — image AND live-widget — UNIFORMLY through the render engine's canvas SCALE TRANSFORM at draw time. A live `fiat_widget`/`bsg_app` tile shrinks at the sides EXACTLY like an image tile: one uniform cover-flow, one scale path, no image-swap hack.
- chosen mechanism: at draw, the widget pushes a scale transform (`scale_i` about the tile's center, [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-MODEL]) and draws the tile's content (an image, or the live child's own draw) under it, then pops. The engine already performs live per-frame transforms — the dradis `rotation`/`speed` bindings are the existing proof that a bound child is drawn under a live transform — so the carousel REUSES that capability rather than inventing scaling.
- reuse-by-reference (extends, never forks): the tile x/scale are bound expressions on `position` over the bindable pixel-stack geometry ([SPEC:CXC-BIND]); the per-tile scale draws through the engine's existing transform path ([SPEC:CXC-RENDER]); the tile content is an ordinary image/child widget over the Canvas contract ([CXC-R-DRAW-CONTRACT]). No new drawing primitive is minted.
- change: "The carousel scales live-widget tiles and image tiles by ONE uniform canvas scale transform, not by pre-rendering discrete image steps."
- context_for_change:
  - prior_state: "The CTk `CarouselView` fakes scale by PRE-RENDERING each image at 11 discrete steps (`SCALE_STEPS`, `SIDE_SCALE`..`CENTER_SCALE`) onto fixed-size canvases and SWAPPING to the nearest step per frame (`_nearest_scale_step`/`set_scale`) — an image-ONLY trick: it cannot scale a live child widget (the CTk build never scaled its `widget_factory` tiles), it quantizes scale to 11 steps, and it costs N×11 pre-rendered bitmaps."
  - reasoning: "The render engine already draws bound children under live transforms (the dradis rotation/speed bindings), and every tile — image or live child — draws over the same Canvas contract. So a single canvas scale transform per tile gives CONTINUOUS (not 11-step) scale, works IDENTICALLY for image and live-widget tiles, and needs no pre-render cache. It is the 'generalize, don't fork' move ([CXC-PRIM-WHY] input-primitive family): one scale path for both tile kinds."
  - rejected_alternative: "Port the CTk pre-render-11-steps-and-swap hack — rejected: it is image-only (cannot scale a live `fiat_widget`/`bsg_app` tile, the exact thing the conversion needs), quantizes to 11 steps, and duplicates a caching layer the engine's transform makes unnecessary. Also rejected: bind each tile's `w`/`h` to a scaled pixel size — that resizes an image fine but does NOT re-scale a live child's internal layout, so it would split image vs live tiles across two scale mechanisms (the seam this decision removes)."
  - invalidating_assumption: "That the render engine's canvas scale transform composes correctly with a live child's own draw on EVERY target back-end. It holds on the Python/Skia tier (the dradis transforms prove it). If a back-end lacks a composable scale transform, that back-end falls back to the reduced image-only variant ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TIER]) rather than forking the desktop mechanism."
  - implication: "One scale path; continuous scale; image and live tiles are visually identical under motion. The `SCALE_STEPS`/pre-render machinery is not ported. This capability leans on the engine transform, which is why the tier position ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TIER]) waives the embedded tier until that transform is confirmed on the C++ back-end."
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TILES], [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TIER]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND] Bindings — `position`/index observable on the bus, center-tap fires a launch intent

- definition: the carousel is bus-native. Three namespaces, all with STATED defaults (a builder MUST NOT guess):
  - `index_ns` (state, PUBLISHED by the widget) — the centered tile's index (and, when the tile carries one, its `target` key/id) is published on every settle so peers know which tile is centered. Default `carousel/index`. SHOULD be set to a caller-meaningful namespace.
  - `goto_ns` (command, CONSUMED by the widget) — publishing an integer index here commands the carousel to snap to that tile (clamped `[0,n−1]`), identical to a swipe. This is what makes the widget drivable headlessly and by an Intelligence (control-is-a-peer, [CXC-CREDO]): "go to tile 2" is a bus publish whether a finger or an Intelligence issues it. Default `carousel/goto`.
  - the `position` float itself lives on a widget-scoped namespace the visual binds to (per [SPEC:CXC-BIND]); the controller ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH]) writes it, the tile x/scale expressions read it.
- center-tap intent: tapping the CENTERED tile ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH] tap rule) fires that tile's activate intent. When a tile carries `target:`, the widget publishes `_app/<target>/open` at global scope — REUSING the launch-bar's intent convention verbatim ([SPEC:CXC-LAUNCHBAR] [CXC-LB-INTENT-BINDING]); it does NOT invent a new activate protocol. A tile with no `target:` publishes a generic `activate_ns` (default `carousel/activate`) carrying the centered index/id, for a non-launcher caller to consume.
- requirement (control-is-a-peer): a human tapping the centered tile and an Intelligence publishing the identical `_app/<target>/open` (or `goto_ns` to first center it, then the activate) are PEERS — neither path is privileged. The widget publishes and returns; it does not itself spawn/launch (widget-owns-intent, [CXC-LB-CREDO]).
- scenario:
  - when: tile 1 (centered, `target: composer`) is tapped, OR an Intelligence publishes `_app/composer/open = true`
  - then: the identical `_app/composer/open` global intent is on the bus; downstream cannot tell the finger from the Intelligence
  - when: an Intelligence publishes `2` to `goto_ns`
  - then: the carousel snaps to tile 2 (ease-out) and publishes `2` (+ tile 2's target) to `index_ns` on settle
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-PARAMS] The full param surface — every knob, every default stated

- definition: the carousel's configuration. Every value below has a STATED default so a builder never guesses; defaults reproduce the CTk `carousel.py` behavior faithfully where one exists.

| Param | Type | Default | Semantics |
|-------|------|---------|-----------|
| `tiles` | list[tile] | — (required) | ordered tile list; each `{image:…}` or `{widget:…}` (+ optional `target:`, `label:`) — [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TILES]. Empty list renders an empty strip + no dots (not an error). |
| `spacing` | float px | `425` | center-to-center distance `S` (CTk `ELEMENT_SPACING`). SHOULD be ≥ a scaled tile width to avoid center/side overlap the caller does not want; not enforced. |
| `tile_width` | float px | `500` | unscaled (center) tile box width (CTk `ELEMENT_BASE_W`). |
| `tile_height` | float px | `500` | unscaled (center) tile box height (CTk `ELEMENT_BASE_H`). |
| `center_scale` | float | `1.0` | scale of the centered tile ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-MODEL]). |
| `side_scale` | float | `0.5` | scale floor for tiles ≥ 1 pane away (CTk `SIDE_SCALE`). |
| `snap_threshold` | float (0..1) | `0.15` | drag past this FRACTION of `spacing` advances one tile (CTk `SNAP_THRESHOLD`). |
| `flick_velocity` | float px/s | `400` | release speed at/above which a flick advances one tile regardless of drift (CTk `velocity_threshold`). |
| `snap_duration_ms` | int | `300` | snap-animation duration (CTk `SNAP_DURATION_MS`). |
| `snap_easing` | enum | `ease_out_cubic` | snap curve `1−(1−t)³` (CTk `_ease_out_cubic`). |
| `elastic_factor` | float (0..1) | `0.30` | edge overshoot multiplier past the first/last tile (CTk `ELASTIC_FACTOR`). |
| `tap_max_drift_px` | float px | `15` | release drift below which (with duration) a release is a TAP not a swipe. |
| `tap_max_duration_ms` | int | `300` | release duration below which (with drift) a release is a TAP. |
| `wrap` | bool | `false` | clamp to `[0,n−1]` (CTk behavior) when false; toroidal wrap when true. |
| `initial_index` | int | `0` | tile centered at load (`position = initial_index`). |
| `show_dots` | bool | `true` | render the one-dot-per-tile indicator row below. |
| `dot_active_color` | hex | accent (`#34c3d0` dark / `#159aa8` light, [CXC-PRIM-TOKENS]) | centered-dot color. (CTk used `#ffffff`; the token accent is the family-consistent default.) |
| `dot_inactive_color` | hex | `#666666` | non-centered dot color (CTk `DOT_INACTIVE_COLOR`). |
| `index_ns` | ns | `carousel/index` | where the centered index/id is published ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]). |
| `goto_ns` | ns | `carousel/goto` | publish an index here to command a snap ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]). |
| `activate_ns` | ns | `carousel/activate` | generic center-tap publish for a tile with no `target:` ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]). |

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TIER] Tier position — WAIVER to console/desktop-tier, with a recorded reason + a reduced-embedded path

- principle: per [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY], a UX capability is not a landed PLATFORM capability until it renders on the ESP32 embedded tier ([SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]) OR is EXPLICITLY scoped console-only with a recorded reason. This node is that explicit scope + reason (the escape hatch), not a silent omission.
- position (recommended): WAIVER — the carousel is scoped CONSOLE/DESKTOP-tier (Python/Skia), NOT embedded, in this spec.
- reason: two independent reasons. (1) FIT: a horizontal touch swipe scale-carousel is a dubious interaction on a 720 px ROUND ESP32 dradis panel — a cover-flow strip wants width and a clear left/right axis a round face does not offer; the interaction, not just the render, is desktop-shaped. (2) MECHANISM: the uniform live-tile scaling ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE]) leans on the engine's canvas scale transform composing with a LIVE child's draw, which is proven on Skia (the dradis rotation/speed transforms) but NOT yet confirmed on the C++ embedded back-end. Scoping console-only now is honest; claiming embedded parity would be the silent-strand anti-pattern tier-parity exists to prevent.
- reduced embedded variant (the escape-hatch's forward path, NOT a port): should an embedded carousel ever be wanted, it is a DISTINCT reduced capability — image-only tiles (no live-widget hosting), the pre-rendered discrete-scale-step trick the CTk build used ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE] prior_state) if the back-end lacks a composable scale transform, and a nav axis suited to the round face (e.g. dot-nav or a shorter fling). It would be its own tier-parity slice, not a mechanical back-end pass of this desktop widget.
- upholds: [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY]
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE]
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

### [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-VERIFY] Acceptance — machine signal + glass gate

- definition: the carousel's two-tier verdict — a machine signal AND, because it is a human-perceptible touch surface, an explicit human/glass gate. A machine-green carousel that has never been dragged on real glass is not accepted.

| ID | Requirement | verify.machine | verify.human (glass) |
|----|-------------|----------------|----------------------|
| CAR-V1 | Geometry is a pure function of `position` ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-MODEL]) | a headless render at `position ∈ {0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0}` asserts each on-screen tile's `x_i = W/2+(i−position)*S` (±0.5 px) and `scale_i` = the clamped falloff (±0.001); center tile lifted above neighbors | on glass: the centered tile reads full-size and lifted; side tiles shrink smoothly toward half-size with distance |
| CAR-V2 | Center-tap fires the activate intent, nothing else ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]) | a test taps the centered tile with an injected bus and asserts EXACTLY ONE publish of the tile's `_app/<target>/open` (or `activate_ns`), no spawn from the widget | on glass: tapping the centered tile launches/activates its target |
| CAR-V3 | Swipe past threshold advances exactly one tile ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH]) | a test drives a release with `dx > 0.15*S` OR `|v|>400` and asserts the target index moves by EXACTLY ±1 in the swipe direction, clamped `[0,n−1]`; `position` settles to the integer target | on glass: a firm swipe/flick advances one tile and snaps home; a flick never skips two |
| CAR-V4 | Sub-threshold swipe snaps back ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH]) | a test drives `|dx| < 0.15*S` AND `|v|<400` and asserts the index is UNCHANGED and `position` eases back to the origin integer | on glass: a small nudge springs back to the same tile |
| CAR-V5 | Drag tracks 1:1; edge rubber-bands ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH]) | a test asserts a pan of `S` px moves `position` by 1.0; past-edge overshoot = `overshoot*elastic_factor`; release snaps inside `[0,n−1]` | on glass: the strip tracks the finger 1:1; past the first/last tile it resists and springs back |
| CAR-V6 | Control-is-a-peer: `goto_ns`/intent publish moves it identically ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-BIND]) | a test publishes an index to `goto_ns` and asserts the carousel snaps + republishes the settled index to `index_ns`, byte-identical to a swipe outcome | n/a (structural) |
| CAR-V7 | State machine: a drag cancels an in-flight snap ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TOUCH]) | a test starts a snap, injects `on_pan_start` mid-animation, and asserts the snap is cancelled and the drag takes over from the live `position` | on glass: grabbing the strip mid-snap immediately follows the finger |
| CAR-V8 | Uniform scale for image AND live tiles ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE]) | a test renders a carousel mixing an image tile and a live-widget tile and asserts both draw at the same `scale_i` for the same distance (one transform path; no `SCALE_STEPS` cache) | on glass: a live `fiat_widget`/`bsg_app` tile shrinks at the sides EXACTLY like an image tile — continuous, no step-popping, no seam between the kinds |
| CAR-V9 | Console-tier scope recorded, not silent ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TIER]) | the spec carries the console/desktop-tier WAIVER node + reason and an UPHOLDS edge to [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY] | n/a (structural) |

- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]

