**Status (0.5, 2026-07-30, [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL])**: DRAFT — spec-only, not yet built. Adds the **carousel** — a touch-driven horizontal scale-carousel (cover-flow), the ComposePlatform equivalent of the CTk launcher's `CarouselView` (`/Maker/launcher/widgets/carousel.py`), authored as nine nodes ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL] + -MODEL/-TOUCH/-TILES/-SCALE/-BIND/-PARAMS/-TIER/-VERIFY). The widget renders entirely from a continuous `position` float on the bus; its VISUAL is a pure bound composite (tile x/scale = bound expressions on `position`), its BEHAVIOR is ONE new interaction-controller seam (the swipe-carousel controller — drag/tap/snap/elastic state machine, the carousel analogue of [CXC-PRIM-DRAGVAL]). TWO tile kinds (static image + live child widget, the CTk `widget_factory` seam). RATIFIED DESIGN DECISION to confirm — [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-SCALE]: scale ALL tiles (image AND live-widget) UNIFORMLY via the render engine's canvas scale transform (the dradis rotation/speed transforms are the proof), NOT the CTk pre-render-11-discrete-steps image-only hack (`change`/`context_for_change` recorded). TIER — [CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-TIER]: recommended WAIVER to console/desktop-tier with a two-part reason (a swipe cover-flow is a dubious fit on a 720px round ESP32; the live-tile scale transform is unconfirmed on the C++ back-end), the embedded case scoped as a distinct reduced image-only variant, not a port. Bindings reuse the launch-bar's `_app/<target>/open` center-tap intent ([CXC-LB-INTENT-BINDING]) and publish the centered index to `index_ns` (+ a `goto_ns` command ns, control-is-a-peer). Full param surface with stated defaults ([CXC-PRIM-CAROUSEL-PARAMS], defaults faithful to `carousel.py`). Awaiting architect ratify (esp. the scale mechanism + the tier waiver) + build (Python/Skia seam + composite).

**Status (0.4, 2026-07-29, [CXC-PRIM-LEDBAR] + [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY])**: DRAFT — spec-only, not yet built. Formalized the segmented LED level-meter as `gauge` `style: "led_bar"` ([CXC-PRIM-LEDBAR], the fourth gauge style beside dial/semicircle/bar): CONFIRMS the shipped baseline (`segments`, `segment_colors`, `orientation`, `reverse`, the lit / 16%-dim hue-true model in `gauge.py:_draw_led_bar`) and ADDS five config params — `segment_shape` (rectangular|square|round) + `segment_long_axis` (across|along, rectangular-only), `segment_gap` (was hardcoded 4px), `padding` (was hardcoded 8px), and `min_segment_size` (short-axis floor, default 0/disabled = backward-compatible). Item (d) "flip the whole module H/V" reconciled onto the existing `orientation`+`reverse` knobs — NO third module-mirror param. Also ratifies [CXC-PRIM-TIER-PARITY] (David's durable ruling): a UX capability is not "done" until it renders on the ESP32 embedded tier over the one narrow-waist Canvas contract ([SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]), or is explicitly `console-only:` with a reason. Defaults keep the current render byte-identical. Awaiting architect ratify + build (Python enhancement, then the C++ embedded-tier port).

**Status (0.3, 2026-07-28, [CXC-PRIM-FADER])**: the linear fill/fader readout landed as `gauge` `style: "bar"` (+ `orientation: vertical|horizontal`) — NOT a new class, NOT a composite — reusing the gauge's namespace/threshold/bus API and Canvas-contract draw. It retires the hand-wired `pixel_stack`+`rect`+`ns()`-geometry throttle calibration fragment: `render/examples/calib_throttle.compose.yaml` is now two `namespace:`-bound bars (throttle/left|right, continuous_0_255) + a title, zero hand-wired geometry exprs. 11 unit tests (`tests/render/test_gauge_bar.py`: fill-fraction monotonicity + ~half-at-midpoint, vertical bottom-up + horizontal left-right origin, threshold warming, measure/style routing, and the calib-scene namespace binding driven over the bus). Pixel proof: `captures/calib_throttle/` (scene at L/R = 0/0, 128/64, 255/200; a 0/128/255 fill strip; a horizontal level meter). Full render suite green (2094 passed; the one failure — `test_agents_skills::test_publisher_reads_real_repo_set` expecting a `console-up` skill — is a pre-existing `.claude` skill-registry drift, orthogonal to this change). Opus tier (composition, not render-systems depth); Fable correctly not used.

**Status (0.2, 2026-07-09)**: OPERATIONAL. All seven composites landed (`render/examples/components/{progress_bar[,_indeterminate],switch,numpad,keyboard,slider,dropdown,segment_bar}.compose.yaml`) — Sonnet built the four pure composites, Opus built slider/dropdown + segment_bar/repeat. **633 tests green macOS**, zero regressions (`test_primitives_composites.py` 20, `test_primitives_interactive.py` 13, `test_component_repeat.py` 14, `test_segment_bar.py` 8). Aesthetic ratified from the pre-vis; Fable correctly not used.

Engine seams REALIZED (each earned its keep, ratified 2026-07-09):
- **[CXC-PRIM-DRAGVAL]** → `render/engine/drag_value.py` (`DragValueController` + declarative `drag_value:` block; base-`Widget` pan delegation). Publish-only, deterministic: dragging to a value and *directly publishing* it land the thumb at the identical pixel (Intelligence "drags" byte-identically to a finger).
- **[CXC-PRIM-POPUP]** → `DialogWidget` list-select mode (`options` + `select_namespace` + `anchor`); reuses the existing overlay, no second system.
- **[CXC-PRIM-REPEAT]** → `render/engine/components.py` (`repeat: N as: i` unrolls at load; composes with `{{param}}`/`use:`/nesting via a `known`-scope token rule that leaves un-owned tokens intact). Generalizes to keyboard/numpad key grids (a queued follow-on refactor).
- **NEW — bindable pixel-stack geometry** (promote into [SPEC:CXC-BIND]): `x/y/w/h` are now bindable (→ `stack_x/y/w/h`), and a *bound* geometry value resolves as ABSOLUTE PIXELS (not the `0..1⇒fraction` shorthand) — this is what makes the slider's visual a pure bound composite.
- **orient vocabulary**: `build_node` now aliases `horizontal→row`, `vertical→column` (`_DIRECTION_ALIASES`), so every primitive accepts David's `horizontal|vertical` terms; segment_bar's param carries that spelling.

Reconciliations / findings:
- spec sketch's `color:` corrected to `fill_color:` (a `rect` has no bindable `color`).
- **string-accumulation gap** (numpad/keyboard): a key publish is last-key-wins, not append — the DSL has no string-concat and there is no read-modify-write publish site outside headless `outputs:` ticks. Per [CXC-BIND-BOUNDARY], a companion bus workload / Intelligence is the intended text accumulator (documented in both configs). A harvested need — candidate for a future text-accumulate site or a DSL string op.
- **binary-engine convention gap** (pre-existing, found via the switch): `publish_value: toggle` hardcodes numeric `0/1`, which the binary state engine's `['off','on']` domain rejects. Worked around (discrete-literal pills); the underlying `toggle`↔binary mismatch is flagged for a runtime fix, not addressed here.
- theme: only dark tokens wired (engine `design.theme` isn't runtime-switchable yet); light hex noted in comments.

## A widget must be able to say "this number is OLD" — staleness is a presentation primitive, not a per-widget afterthought [CXC-PRIM-STALE]

- requirement: Every widget bound to a bus namespace renders a STALENESS TREATMENT when the receiver's verdict is not fresh. ⛔ The treatment is OPT-OUT, not opt-in: `Widget.staleness_treatment` defaults to `"auto"`; a widget declines with `"none"` or renders its own with `"self"`, and declares it as `staleness:` in `.compose.yaml`. It is drawn in `engine/layout.draw_child` — the same choke point the opacity/rotate/scale transforms compose through — so there is exactly ONE path that dims.
- why-opt-out-not-opt-in: MEASURED 2026-08-15: 53 widget classes implement `on_bus_state`, and 22 wrapped a coercion in a handler that swallowed the unknown identically — `try: self.value = float(value) except (TypeError, ValueError): pass`, where `UnknownValueError` subclasses `TypeError`, so the handler CATCHES IT AND PASSES. 22 silent swallowers is the measured cost of a mechanism each author must remember. Opt-out makes the default correct and lets a widget deliberately decline.
- the-failure-this-prevents: ⛔ NOT a blank and NOT a zero — SILENT RETENTION OF THE STALE NUMBER, which is strictly worse than either because the display is confidently wrong and nothing anywhere reports it. Measured on a live GaugeWidget: publisher alive sends 3 -> gauge.value 3.0; publisher DEAD, receiver correctly says UNKNOWN -> gauge.value STILL 3.0. Field instance the same day: a Dradis console showed `contacts: 3 threats: 0` from a publisher that had stopped. The number was TRUE when written; the display had no way to say it was old.
- presentation-three-cues-never-a-blank: RETAIN the last value, DIM it (0.30-0.45), HATCH it at 45 degrees surface-anchored, BADGE it. ⛔ NEVER BLANK: a blanked bar gauge reads as ZERO and a blanked LED reads as OFF — a viewer's default reading of an empty instrument is a MEASUREMENT, so blanking converts "confidently wrong" into "confidently wrong AND alarming", and discards the most useful thing a dead channel can say: it was 3, forty seconds ago. Each cue alone FAILS: dimming is a cue of DEGREE and a glance does not read degree; a hatch alone reads as decoration on a dense card; a badge alone is what a scanning eye skips.
- badge-must-degrade-not-vanish: ⛔ The badge degrades to a 7px corner wedge when the widget is too small for a pill. "Too small for the badge" must never become "silently untreated" — that is this entire defect class reappearing at small sizes.
- four-verdicts-not-two: STALE (amber, single hatch, WITH age) — one publisher died. NODE_SILENT (red CROSS-hatch, NO age) — a board died; cross-hatch so the distinction survives a colour-blind operator, and no age because a namespace's age proves nothing about a quiet namespace. NO_SIGNAL (slate, no age) — never arrived, may be booting. NO_DATA (blue-grey, sparse, DELIBERATELY THE GENTLEST) — the publisher is alive and honestly does not know; an alarm here trains the operator to ignore alarms. ⛔ They look different because their REMEDIES differ.
- undeclared-authorises-no-verdict: ⛔ A namespace with no declared cadence renders UNTREATED, byte-identical to its live frame. This is deliberate and load-bearing: without it, adding this mechanism would have hatched every widget in every existing scene overnight. The question was never asked, so it is not answered.
- quiet-by-design-must-not-be-treated: ⛔ The inverse defect is as real as the original. A namespace declared `quiet_by_construction` — the throttle's "quiet-bus by construction" — renders BYTE-IDENTICAL after 100,000 s of silence. A treatment that fires here marks the fleet's most-watched namespace dead seconds after the operator releases the lever. Both directions require a negative control.
- absorb-private-freshness-notions-only-where-they-are-transport-age: `link_health` hand-rolled a monotonic stamp and a 12 s threshold — the freshness receiver rebuilt inside one widget — and was ABSORBED. ⛔ `lts_engine_rail` and `decisions_summary` were NOT, correctly: their `fresh` comes from the PUBLISHED PAYLOAD (index currency, newest decision session stamp) — a CONTENT fact, not a transport age. Conflating them would make "index up to date" and "message arrived recently" the same word. Both now get the transport half free from the default treatment, which incidentally fixed a second latent bug: a dead lts-engine would have left a frozen "UP FRESH".
- two-defects-only-eyes-found: The badge first read `NODE DOWN 0s` — the NAMESPACE's age, precisely the number that proves nothing about a quiet namespace. And `fill_path` with tuple-shaped commands matches NO case in the Skia backend's structural `match` and draws an empty path SILENTLY — the corner wedge was not painting at all while the test passed on the hatch alone. ⛔ Neither was visible to any machine signal; both were found by looking at pixels.
- implementation: python/composeio/render/freshness_view.py; python/composeio/render/engine/layout.py:draw_child; python/composeio/render/widget.py:staleness_treatment
- status: built (Python render tier) — ⛔ MECHANISM SUPERSEDED 2026-08-15, rework to bindings OWED. The visual language is ratified and stands; the `draw_child` code path and the `staleness_treatment` widget attribute are the wrong form and must not be copied. Read `mechanism-superseded-2026-08-15` on this node BEFORE implementing anything from it.
- verify: [CXC-PRIM-STALE-TESTS]
- cpp-tier-is-a-named-followon: ⛔ `cyd/src/compose_scene.cpp` does NOT draw this. The treatment uses only existing Canvas-contract primitives so the narrow waist is NOT widened — this is a BACKEND extended to meet the contract, not a widget forked to a backend — but until ported, an embedded panel shows the same stale number this node exists to end.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-PRIM]
- see-also: [CXC-PRIM-COMPOSITES], [DEC-A-FRESHNESS-VERDICT-IS-STATE-NOT-A-HANDLER], [DX-A-NEW-FACT-IS-STATE-NEVER-A-HANDLER]
- mechanism-superseded-2026-08-15: ⛔ THE VISUAL LANGUAGE BELOW STANDS; THE PLUMBING IT DESCRIBES IS THE WRONG FORM AND MUST NOT BE COPIED. As first authored, this node specified the treatment as CODE drawn from `engine/layout.draw_child` with an opt-out `Widget.staleness_treatment` attribute — an O(1) base-class hook. David ruled that design wrong the same day: "we shouldn't have to PROGRAM for that, it should just HAPPEN because a new state has been emitted." The freshness verdict is published ONCE as STATE on the internal bus, and every reaction — a dimmed widget, an LED, a relay failing safe — is an ORDINARY BINDING over that namespace. The correct amount of new reaction code is ZERO. See [DEC-A-FRESHNESS-VERDICT-IS-STATE-NOT-A-HANDLER] and [DX-A-NEW-FACT-IS-STATE-NEVER-A-HANDLER], which names O(1) "the seductive wrong answer" because it passes every DRY review and is still code, and code lives in ONE TIER. WHAT SURVIVES UNCHANGED: four verdicts, never-blank, retain-dim-hatch-badge, the badge degrading to a wedge, undeclared-authorises-no-verdict, and quiet-by-construction. Those are presentation decisions and they were right. WHAT DIES: `staleness_treatment` as a widget attribute, the `draw_child` choke point as the single dimming path, and the `on_stale:` actuator vocabulary (withdrawn). THE TELL THAT PROVED IT: the O(1) fix had already generated its own named follow-on — a C++ port — and under O(0) there is nothing to port, because the embedded tier already consumes state. Empirically confirmed: `ns()` reads any namespace, an expression's parse-time namespace list IS its subscription list, colour swap / `visible` / `led.state` / `text` all bind today, and an unset namespace reads falsy so it fails safe. ONE GENUINE GAP: no opacity/brightness attribute on the leaf, so "dim" is a colour swap there. Reason through dusk gift P13 before implementing.
